<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#rajasuya">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rajasuya</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Rajasuya was a Vedic royal consecration ritual in which a king was anointed and proclaimed his supremacy over other rulers. Described in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yajurveda">Yajurveda</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmanas">Brahmanas</a>, it involved a long series of ceremonies, including a mock <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> raid and a game of dice. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahabharata">Mahabharata</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yudhishthira">Yudhishthira</a>'s Rajasuya sets off the fateful dice game that leads to war.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rajasuya.jpg" alt="Rajasuya" width="320" height="227" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Himachal Pradesh, Kangra Workshop · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:King_Yudhisthira_Performs_the_Rajasuya_Sacrifice.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
